--TEST--
`Core\Command::run` converts an argument declared at a subset of an enum's cases by the case's own name, exactly as the whole enum is — `rule:tooling/commands-are-compiled`, `rule:enums/no-class-machinery`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 3 admits a union of an enum's cases wherever § 6 admits the enum, and the
// two are one conversion read twice: `nvs_types::commands::ArgConv::Enum`
// carries the subset as the same word/value pairs the whole enum's row does,
// with the cases the union did not name dropped. So `Warn` writes the same word
// here as in `command-run-converts-an-enum-argument-by-its-case-name` and
// arrives as the same backing integer, which is what stops a subset and its
// enum from coming to disagree about a case they both admit.
//
// The backing is deliberately neither the case's position nor a run of ones:
// `Warn` is 10 in a three-case enum whose first case is 1, so a conversion
// handing back an index — or the position within the *subset* — would answer
// with a plausible small number rather than with this one.
enum Level: uint {
    Quiet = 1,
    Warn = 10,
    Error = 20,
}

class Logs {
    #[Core\Command(name: "emit", about: "Write one line at a level")]
    public static function emit(Level::Warn|Level::Error $level): uint {
        echo "level: ", $level as uint, "\n";
        return 0;
    }
}

// The status is taken first, because the handler writes while `run` is still
// being evaluated.
uint $status = Core\Command::run();
echo "status: ", $status, "\n";
--ARGS--
emit
Warn
--EXPECT--
level: 10
status: 0
